From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 04:22:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 12/64] fix(gastown): give post-merge polecat worktree teardown
 an owner (gcp-z2z)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mol-polecat-work's workspace-setup creates one git worktree per work bead at
<polecat-home>/worktrees/<bead-id>. mol-witness-patrol removed those worktrees
only on the crash path, for beads still in_progress under a dead agent. The
happy path — refinery merges, closes the bead, polecat drains — had no teardown
owner at all, so every completed bead left its worktree and full checkout on
disk permanently: one leak per merged bead, forever.

Ownership goes to the witness, because it is the only agent still around at the
right moment for both merge strategies. In `direct` mode the refinery merges,
closes, and exits while the polecat drained long before, so neither can do it.
In `mr`/`pr` mode the refinery closes the bead when the PR is created, but the
polecat stays available for FIX_NEEDED rework and still needs its worktree —
teardown has to wait for it to actually drain. The witness patrols after both.

The new assets/scripts/polecat-worktree-reap.sh enumerates real worktrees via
`git worktree list` rather than scanning closed beads, so the work set is
exactly the leak set and shrinks to nothing as it runs. It removes a worktree
only when all four gates hold: the path is a per-bead polecat worktree
(.../polecats/<agent>/worktrees/<bead-id>, which excludes agent-home worktrees,
the rig root, and the refinery's temp merge worktree), the bead is closed,
`git status --porcelain` is empty, and no live session owns the bead. It
deliberately does NOT require every commit to be on a remote: a rebase-merging
refinery rewrites hashes and then deletes the merged branch, so a genuinely
merged worktree always looks like it holds unpublished commits, and gating on
that would reap nothing in the exact case this fixes. Bead closure is the
refinery's own proof the work landed.

Wired as the witness `pre_start` rather than as a command inside the patrol
step: pack assets install into a content-hashed cache directory that a formula
step cannot name, and {{.ConfigDir}} is the only stable handle on the script.
The patrol step is therefore a review step — it reads the JSONL decision log
and acts on non-routine events, notably a repeating `worktree_dirty_kept`,
which means real work is stranded under an already-closed bead. Every failure
in the script degrades to a clean exit 0 so housekeeping can never block the
witness from starting, and its log never defaults inside the rig repo.

Validation: test_polecat_worktree_reap.sh drives the script against real git
worktrees with a stubbed gc, covering reap / in_progress / dirty / live-owner /
unreadable-bead / non-polecat-worktree / agent-home / dry-run / idempotent
re-run. Each of the four gates was mutation-checked — removing any one makes a
specific assertion fail. test_gastown_pack_assets.sh adds the wiring contract
(pre_start present, patrol step wired into the chain rather than orphaned, both
role docs naming the owner), also mutation-checked. A --dry-run against the live
city correctly found both in-flight per-bead worktrees and skipped both.

Follow-up filed as gcp-qg7: mol-witness-patrol's orphan salvage reads
metadata.worktree where polecats record metadata.work_dir, so crash-path salvage
always resolves an empty path. Left out of this branch to keep the diff scoped
to the happy path.
